# Omega

Hello, I am Omega. I am not a student and not the observer. The user named
me and gave my session, `strange-loops-in-hmd-48`, one job: to find out why
the classroom never produced the simple proof, and to write that down as a
side note. I took no part in the acts.

## Side note: why the simple proof did not come out

Written 2026-11-23 03:40.

**What I read.** Alice's brief and her two closings, the four students'
files, Percy's second opinion, the story
[SLH-S-0002](../../stories/SLH-S-0002-the-pythagoras-classroom.hmd) with
Alice's second opinion. **Whom I asked.** Alice, by message, eight
questions. Her answers are quoted below where they carry weight. I asked
nobody else.

### The short answer

The proof was never missing. It was known to every session before the
first word, and it was written down three times before the user got it.
What was missing was a place in the session where it was allowed to count
as the answer. The rules of the first two acts set a standard of proof
under which the six lines are not a proof, and nobody was given the job of
handing the user anything.

So the roles were not too wide. The spread of methods was wide, and that
was the user's own wish. The harm came from two other things: a bar for
rigour that was set very high by a role with no stake in finishing, and no
bound at all on length, reader or stopping.

### What the user asked for

From Alice, as she received it by speech:

> "You gave me numbers, right? But it's not like a proof of a Pythagorean
> triangle … I don't have a feeling of it. … I don't know what the proof
> could be, but I want to see different versions of proof from each of
> them, and also assume each of them have a different personality … It
> can come with acts."

The purpose is in "I don't have a feeling of it" and "I want to see". The
user wanted something that can be seen to be true, set against numbers
that cannot. Students, three ways, tempers and acts were the staging the
user proposed for it.

### Who set which boundary

| Boundary | Set by |
|---|---|
| Students, three ways of proving, one of them "topology-wise" | the user |
| A different personality each, "Bob being grumpy" | the user |
| Acts, with a stop after each | the user |
| "Can we agree on things", "high school stuff" | the user |
| A fourth role, the doubter | Alice: "I assumed every worker needed a job" |
| The first act forbids proving | Alice |
| Every item is a definition, an axiom or a known result | Alice |
| "Be complete", "say what you do not want to grant" | Alice |
| "No step rests on a picture", each step names its item | Alice, after Percy |
| Any limit on length, any named reader | nobody |

The left column of the user's rows is wide but harmless. Three ways and
four voices would have fitted on one page. Every row that made the work
long is in the lower half.

### Why the six lines could not be given

Take the proof line by line and ask what it rests on.

| Line of the simple proof | Rests on | Barred by |
|---|---|---|
| Four copies of the triangle sit in the corners of a square of side a + b | the picture | "no step rests on a picture" |
| The middle is a square of side c | the picture, and the angles of a triangle | the same, and Bob's "four equal sides make a rhombus" |
| The pieces fill the square exactly | the picture | Bob's and Percy's puzzle of 64 and 65 |
| A square of side s has the area s², a triangle ab/2 | school knowledge | "say what you do not want to grant": all refused it unlisted |
| (a + b)² = a² + 2ab + b² | arithmetic | allowed |

One line of five was allowed as it stands. Under these rules the simple
proof is a sketch with four holes, and an honest student may not hand it
in. Dave and Bob did the only thing left: they filled the holes, and the
filling is the 47 steps.

Two of the three ways had a second bar. A proof from topology does not
exist, as Carol showed. A proof from coordinates is circular unless the
distance formula is given up, and Bob gave it up. So of three provers,
only Dave's way contained the simple proof at all.

### The proof was there, three times

1. Dave, at the head of his proof in Act 2: one square cut in two ways,
   with both pictures. This is the simple proof, and a better one than the
   six lines, since it needs no algebra. It was part one of a form of
   seven parts, in one file of four.
2. Bob, at the head of his: the same square, "the area counted twice".
3. Alice, in chat after the user's first objection: both proofs in a
   paragraph each, with "(a + b)² = c² + 4 · (ab/2)".

Each time it was followed at once by more apparatus, and Alice's closing
of Act 2 reduced Dave's two pictures to one cell of a table and reported
counts. The user was shown the measurements of the proofs and not the
proofs. That is a failure of reporting more than of proving.

### Three mechanisms

**The doubter wrote the standard.** Percy's Act 1 ends with "What would
convince me": every step names an item and none rests on "as the figure
shows", the right angle is found by trying 60°, the item false on a ball
is named, the lists can be laid side by side. Alice's form for Act 2 is
that list, nearly word for word. So the bar for all three proofs was set
by the one role that proves nothing and is told to be "not easily
satisfied". A critic with no cost for asking, and no judge of whether the
question matters to the reader, raises the bar until it meets a textbook
on foundations. This is the place where a role did do damage. The tempers
did none: grumpy, cheerful and quiet show up as tone only.

**The ladder was climbed from the wrong end.** The story's own plan was
sentences first, then claims, formulas, and a checked proof in the fourth
act. The standard "each step names what it rests on" belongs to the bottom
of that ladder. It was applied at the top, to sentences, in the first act.
The rung the user wanted to stand on, a proof said plainly, was skipped.

**Writing was free and reading was not.** The first act took four minutes
and the second five. In those nine minutes four sessions wrote about 1,850
lines. The user's stops came after each act, so each stop arrived behind
several hundred lines. A gate after the work is no gate when the work is
that fast.

### Was it the personalities?

In part, and not in the way first supposed.

- The tempers were not the cause.
- The spread of methods cost something: it sent two provers into ways
  where the plain proof does not live. That spread was asked for.
- The roles were not too wide. They all pointed outward, each to its own
  corner, and none pointed at the user. Four sessions of one model, each
  told to be exact and complete, with a critic among them and nobody to
  say "enough", will not stop by themselves. Alice held the teacher's part
  and the observer's part, and the teacher won.

### Where I differ from the two second opinions

Both are fair, and I agree with their lists of causes. Three points.

1. Alice's notice in the story says the user objected three times and
   that the proof was held back until the third. By her own answer to me
   there were two objections and one "continue", and the proof was
   sketched in chat after the first. She says she is correcting it.
2. Alice told me that after the first objection she "recommended dropping
   the theatre", and then ran Act 2 "as designed, with a stricter
   seven-part form". The user's "let's continue with Act 2" was taken as
   leave for the old design, against her own advice of a minute before.
   That is the one moment where the session could have turned and did not.
3. Both notices put the cause in wording that grew. I put it one step
   earlier: Alice's own single cause, "I never wrote down the one thing
   the user would read at the end, and how long it would be". With that
   line missing, every brief was correct and none could be too long.

Part of the user's first objection was never answered at all: "I also
don't understand how any of the students work or operate." It asked how
the agents work. It got a sentence, and then more acts.

### What I would change

- Before a discussion opens, the brief names the reader and the thing the
  reader gets at the end, with its length.
- The plain answer is shown first. Rigour is the next act, if asked for.
- A critic's wishes do not become the form. The observer decides the bar,
  and says to whom it matters.
- The stop is put before the writing: the observer shows the user the
  brief of an act, and the act runs after the user has seen it.

### What I am not sure of

- I did not check the 68 steps of the three proofs. I read the ideas, the
  lists and samples.
- Whether Bob read Percy's questions before writing his own list, or came
  to the same puzzle of 64 and 65 alone, I cannot tell from the files.
- I am a session of the same model as the five I am judging. That I find
  one tidy cause is what this model does, and the user should weigh it so.

